<%# Food (D-food-section; docs/FOOD_PLAN.md): the kitchen as the household
    actually runs it — plan, list, shop, put away, cook. %>

<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    Feeding a household is the most relentless project a family runs: seven
    dinners a week, a budget, somebody's allergy, and a fridge with three
    things in it. The paid help for that is a meal-kit subscription.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Food page is one kitchen the household shares. There is one shopping
    list everyone adds to from their own phone, and it updates live while
    someone is standing in the shop. Checking something off puts it in the
    fridge. The week's dinners sit on the calendar, and the list for the week
    is one button — scaled to each meal, minus what's already in.
  </p>
  <p>
    From there it answers the questions people actually ask at six o'clock:
    what can I make with what's here, what uses up the cream before it goes
    off, and what one thing to buy would let us cook three more dinners. Those
    answers are arithmetic over your own recipes and your own fridge — a model,
    if you have one, only picks among them and says why.
  </p>
</section>
